Our mobile load speed was terrible until we audited our asset pipeline.

Most of our users visit our store on their phones. A three-second delay in page load means losing a customer before they even see our products.

I ran a Lighthouse audit on our e-commerce site and was shocked by the performance score. Huge unoptimized hero images were blocking the main thread, and our bundle size was bloated with unused JavaScript.

I spent the weekend converting all our product images to WebP format and setting up dynamic lazy loading. I also replaced a couple of heavy npm packages with lighter vanilla JS alternatives.

Reducing our main bundle size by 40% made the site feel incredibly snappy on mobile. It was a good reminder that fancy animations do not matter if your site takes forever to load on a basic connection.

What is your go-to trick for cutting down initial page load times on mobile?

